window.docContents[83].resolve({"/Abstraction/Lambdas-and-function-values/#Core-C______--Abstraction--Lambdas-and-function-values":{"contents":"A lambda has no type of its own. The judgment ,  is acceptable at , checks a lambda against the function type expected where it occurs, the argument of new std::function<F>(λ) in . For any other expression it is  with .The type checker checks the body under  with every variable of the enclosing scope marked read only, the copies of [=]. The parameters of the lambda are ordinary variables, and the result type of the body is the result type  of the lambda.The rule T-LocVar requires a variable that is not read only, so an assignment to a captured variable, or a reference to it, is a type error.Anywhere else a lambda is a syntax error, since the grammar admits it only as an argument of new. As the argument of a new of a class of the program it is a type error, since no constructor parameter has a function type. The grammar chapter, , explains the decision.\n\nA lambda evaluates to a closure with copies of the variables of its body that the environment binds, its parameters excluded, taken when the lambda is evaluated. The closure holds values, not locations. A captured int or bool is a copy the body reads and never writes. A captured pointer still reaches its object in , so an effect through it is visible outside the lambda.\n\nA call through a function object, (*f)(e₁, …, eₖ) for a pointer f, evaluates the callee to the object and reads its target, a closure. It then evaluates the arguments by value and left to right, and allocates fresh locations for the captured copies and for the parameters. The body runs in an environment with those bindings only, and the return frees them with the locals of the body.When the callee is a reference g to a function object, g(…) is this rule and not the call of the function named g.The target  is a closure, and the function with no target,  at , has no derivation, so its call is error in the evaluator. The application of the closure to the values is a judgment of its own. It allocates the captured copies and then the arguments, allocMany of , and its body ends as the body of  does.\n\n","context":"Core C++\u0009Abstraction","header":"5.3. Lambdas and function values","id":"/Abstraction/Lambdas-and-function-values/#Core-C______--Abstraction--Lambdas-and-function-values"}});